{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790463106,"nonce":"CoMTu1g2CRz1yZy5o99lhWG/iiA=","title":"wasm32-wasip1-threads builds refused at link since 30d244b","message":"`ASH_WASM_FIBERS=1 ash --build m.wasm --target wasm32-wasip1-threads m.hl`\nfails:\n\n    the linked module would import 1 symbol(s) no host can supply:\n    wasi.thread-spawn. These come from the runtime object, so it is older\n    than the compiler that emitted the calls.\n\nThe message is wrong about the cause: the runtime object was fresh.\n`host_can_supply` (ash_wasm_link) accepts `wasi_*` modules only, and\nwasi-threads' import lives in module `wasi`. Both hosts answer it\n(native/threads.rs, browser/imports.rs). `ash wasm --validate` refuses the\nsame modules for the same reason, and for `env.memory`, the shared memory a\nthreaded module imports and its host makes.\n\nEvery threaded module in examples/browser predates 30d244b (Sep 7 vs\nSep 10); nothing builds the threads target in CI, so it went unnoticed.\n\nAlso on the same build path: a wasm build that names an HDLL printed the\nnative \"takes the SHARED runtime\" note and \"wasm cannot load one\", although\na side module beside the output is loaded.","files":null}]}